BRANCH=E2=80=94TEST 1. UK grid-connection queue change-history =E2=80=94 st=
rongest seed

*What changes/disappears:* project rows, capacities, connection dates, gate
status, ownership/naming and queue composition. Gridview reports NESO=E2=80=
=99s TEC
register snapshot of 18 Sep 2026 at 2,200 rows / 1,705 active queue
projects and says the register updates Tuesdays and Fridays. Government
says transmission demand applications grew 460% in six months to June 2025
and waits can reach 15 years; Ofgem says demand applications surged from
41GW to 125GW in under a year, largely data centres. This is not a cosmetic
dataset: queue state is becoming an economic bottleneck.

*Raw sources:* NESO TEC register / related DNO registers; Ofgem connections
consultations; planning applications; Companies House; project/developer
sites; future grid-reform decisions.

*Canonical key:* persistent project identity built from register identifier
where present + site/geospatial identity + developer/owner + technology +
connection point. Preserve aliases and temporal ownership.

*Transformation:* turn snapshots into a GRID_PROJECT_EVENT stream:
entered_queue, capacity_changed, date_moved, gate_changed, owner_changed,
planning_advanced, planning_failed, connected, disappeared. Derive queue_ag=
e,
date_slippage, survival_probability, speculation_score, MW_at_risk,
constraint_pressure[grid_node], and eventually empirical connection-time
distributions by technology/region/developer.

*One-year asset:* ~100 twice-weekly snapshots plus project-level event
history through an unusually important reform period. The transformed event
history is materially harder to reconstruct than the current register.

*Buyers/decisions:* developers, data-centre site selectors, BESS/renewables
developers, grid consultants, infrastructure funds, lenders and
land/property investors deciding where projects can actually connect and
which nominal queue positions are credible.

*Counterevidence:* Gridview already snapshots/displays the queue and
incumbents may retain old NESO files. Therefore raw TEC archives alone are
not a moat. The moat must be cross-source temporal identity +
planning/company evidence + probabilistic viability/constraint metrics.

*Legal/access:* public official registers are the preferred spine; preserve
source licensing/provenance per snapshot and avoid relying on third-party
redistribution rights.

*Smallest experiment:* save every official TEC/DNO snapshot for 30 days;
entity-resolve changes; test whether we can explain every row delta and
whether project-level changes are absent or cumbersome in public historical
access.

*Falsifier:* NESO launches a complete versioned project-history API with
stable IDs and historical states, or an incumbent cheaply sells the same
cross-source event graph.

*Evidence graph:* CONFIRMED: queue is huge, changing and economically
consequential. CONFIRMED: TEC file is updated twice weekly. INFERRED:
buyers will pay specifically for our transformed history. UNKNOWN: exact
completeness of retrievable historic snapshots.
2. UK planning application event/history graph

Commercial evidence is unusually strong. PlanNexus advertises a live
normalized API across 375+ authorities; PlanningAPI UK advertises 2.4M+
applications across 400+ councils and a monitoring API/webhooks aimed at
loan portfolios. That directly validates a paying use case for normalized
planning-change data. Meanwhile the official Planning Data service exposes
100+ datasets, but its planning-application dataset currently shows only
100,627 records, says its collector last ran in Sep 2025, and states the
data was created by MHCLG pending replacement with authoritative sources.
Official quarterly statistics are aggregated, not a full project event
archive.

*What disappears:* validation dates, document/status changes, amendments,
objections/consultations, decision timing, conditions and council-portal
state; local portals are fragmented and mutable.

*Transformation:* entity-resolve application =E2=86=92 site =E2=86=92 parce=
l/address =E2=86=92
applicant/developer/company =E2=86=92 project type =E2=86=92 grid node. Pre=
serve every
status/document delta and derive approval_velocity, decision_latency,
amendment_count, project_survival, local_supply_pipeline, developer_hit_rat=
e,
constraint_events.

*Buyers:* property lenders, developers, brokers, site selectors, insurers,
local-market intelligence, construction suppliers. Existing monitoring
products prove that planning changes affect real decisions.

*Counterevidence:* this is already a commercial category;
PlanNexus/PlanningAPI may have substantial history. We should not build
another generic planning API. Our wedge is *temporal economic
transformation* and joins into UKGraph/grid/Companies House.

*Smallest experiment:* choose 3 councils + 100 economically meaningful
applications; snapshot daily for 30 days; measure event yield, portal
reproducibility and how many useful transitions are not represented in
official aggregate history.

*Falsifier:* commercial providers expose cheap complete point-in-time
application/document history with equivalent entity joins.
