Buyer / willingness-to-pay analysis

Strongest direct commercial validation this run: planning monitoring APIs,
EV charging data APIs, and used-car data feeds. MarketCheck openly prices
UK listing/history API calls and sells custom daily/weekly/monthly feeds
over 600k+ listings. This proves businesses pay for normalized ephemeral
marketplace state=E2=80=94but also *kills used cars as a fresh garden* unle=
ss we
discover a novel transformation or neglected submarket. PlanningAPI
explicitly pitches status monitoring for loan portfolios, an excellent
example of the buyer-decision test. Zapmap sells live availability data to
business integrations.

Grid queue has weaker direct price-page evidence but much stronger economic
constraint evidence: multi-year connection waits and active
government/Ofgem intervention make queue credibility directly relevant to
capital allocation.
Reconstruction test

*Grid queue: PASS, conditional.* Current registers can be saved, but
complete cross-source project identity and reform-era state transitions are
not obviously available as a canonical historical product. Start now.

*Planning events: PASS, but competitive.* Council portals mutate and
official central coverage is incomplete. However commercial aggregators
exist. Only plant if our transformed temporal graph is differentiated.

*EV charger reliability: PASS on raw ephemerality; CONDITIONAL on
competitive/access moat.* Minute-level availability is intrinsically
ephemeral, but incumbents may already own it.

*Care capacity: WEAK PASS.* Provider/current availability may be ephemeral,
but official aggregate history is increasingly backfilled.

*Livestock microstructure: TEST.* Sale reports may persist, but lot-level
pre-sale catalogues, withdrawals and bid/clearance dynamics could
disappear. Need source audit.
