1. UK grid-connection queue change-history =E2=80=94 strongest seed
<p><strong>What changes/disappears:</strong> project rows, capacities, conn=
ection dates, gate status, ownership/naming and queue composition. Gridview=
 reports NESO=E2=80=99s TEC register snapshot of 18 Sep 2026 at 2,200 rows =
/ 1,705 active queue projects and says the register updates Tuesdays and Fr=
idays. Government says transmission demand applications grew 460% in six mo=
nths to June 2025 and waits can reach 15 years; Ofgem says demand applicati=
ons surged from 41GW to 125GW in under a year, largely data centres. This i=
s not a cosmetic dataset: queue state is becoming an economic bottleneck.</=
p>
<p><strong>Raw sources:</strong> NESO TEC register / related DNO registers;=
 Ofgem connections consultations; planning applications; Companies House; p=
roject/developer sites; future grid-reform decisions.</p>
<p><strong>Canonical key:</strong> persistent project identity built from r=
egister identifier where present + site/geospatial identity + developer/own=
er + technology + connection point. Preserve aliases and temporal ownership=
.</p>
<p><strong>Transformation:</strong> turn snapshots into a <code>GRID_PROJEC=
T_EVENT</code> stream: entered_queue, capacity_changed, date_moved, gate_ch=
anged, owner_changed, planning_advanced, planning_failed, connected, disapp=
eared. Derive <code>queue_age</code>, <code>date_slippage</code>, <code>sur=
vival_probability</code>, <code>speculation_score</code>, <code>MW_at_risk<=
/code>, <code>constraint_pressure[grid_node]</code>, and eventually empiric=
al connection-time distributions by technology/region/developer.</p>
<p><strong>One-year asset:</strong> ~100 twice-weekly snapshots plus projec=
t-level event history through an unusually important reform period. The tra=
nsformed event history is materially harder to reconstruct than the current=
 register.</p>
<p><strong>Buyers/decisions:</strong> developers, data-centre site selector=
s, BESS/renewables developers, grid consultants, infrastructure funds, lend=
ers and land/property investors deciding where projects can actually connec=
t and which nominal queue positions are credible.</p>
<p><strong>Counterevidence:</strong> Gridview already snapshots/displays th=
e queue and incumbents may retain old NESO files. Therefore raw TEC archive=
s alone are not a moat. The moat must be cross-source temporal identity + p=
lanning/company evidence + probabilistic viability/constraint metrics.</p>
<p><strong>Legal/access:</strong> public official registers are the preferr=
ed spine; preserve source licensing/provenance per snapshot and avoid relyi=
ng on third-party redistribution rights.</p>
<p><strong>Smallest experiment:</strong> save every official TEC/DNO snapsh=
ot for 30 days; entity-resolve changes; test whether we can explain every r=
ow delta and whether project-level changes are absent or cumbersome in publ=
ic historical access.</p>
<p><strong>Falsifier:</strong> NESO launches a complete versioned project-h=
istory API with stable IDs and historical states, or an incumbent cheaply s=
ells the same cross-source event graph.</p>
<p><strong>Evidence graph:</strong> CONFIRMED: queue is huge, changing and =
economically consequential. CONFIRMED: TEC file is updated twice weekly. IN=
FERRED: buyers will pay specifically for our transformed history. UNKNOWN: =
exact completeness of retrievable historic snapshots.</p>

2. UK planning application event/history graph
<p>Commercial evidence is unusually strong. PlanNexus advertises a live nor=
malized API across 375+ authorities; PlanningAPI UK advertises 2.4M+ applic=
ations across 400+ councils and a monitoring API/webhooks aimed at loan por=
tfolios. That directly validates a paying use case for normalized planning-=
change data. Meanwhile the official Planning Data service exposes 100+ data=
sets, but its planning-application dataset currently shows only 100,627 rec=
ords, says its collector last ran in Sep 2025, and states the data was crea=
ted by MHCLG pending replacement with authoritative sources. Official quart=
erly statistics are aggregated, not a full project event archive.</p>
<p><strong>What disappears:</strong> validation dates, document/status chan=
ges, amendments, objections/consultations, decision timing, conditions and =
council-portal state; local portals are fragmented and mutable.</p>
<p><strong>Transformation:</strong> entity-resolve application =E2=86=92 si=
te =E2=86=92 parcel/address =E2=86=92 applicant/developer/company =E2=86=92=
 project type =E2=86=92 grid node. Preserve every status/document delta and=
 derive <code>approval_velocity</code>, <code>decision_latency</code>, <cod=
e>amendment_count</code>, <code>project_survival</code>, <code>local_supply=
_pipeline</code>, <code>developer_hit_rate</code>, <code>constraint_events<=
/code>.</p>
<p><strong>Buyers:</strong> property lenders, developers, brokers, site sel=
ectors, insurers, local-market intelligence, construction suppliers. Existi=
ng monitoring products prove that planning changes affect real decisions.</=
p>
<p><strong>Counterevidence:</strong> this is already a commercial category;=
 PlanNexus/PlanningAPI may have substantial history. We should not build an=
other generic planning API. Our wedge is <em>temporal economic transformati=
on</em> and joins into UKGraph/grid/Companies House.</p>
<p><strong>Smallest experiment:</strong> choose 3 councils + 100 economical=
ly meaningful applications; snapshot daily for 30 days; measure event yield=
, portal reproducibility and how many useful transitions are not represente=
d in official aggregate history.</p>
<p><strong>Falsifier:</strong> commercial providers expose cheap complete p=
oint-in-time application/document history with equivalent entity joins.</p>

